{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11978 - MONDO_grouped_18874_4643",
  "verbalization": "Glasdegib is associated with Myeloid Leukemia through a series of indications that link it to Mitoxantrone, a drug known to be effective in treating Myeloid Leukemia. The evidence suggests that Glasdegib may be repurposed for use in patients with Myeloid Leukemia, particularly those with specific genetic mutations such as Npm1 or Inv3(p21;q26.2). These mutations are associated with a subset of Myeloid Leukemia cases, and the shared indication with Mitoxantrone implies a potential mechanistic overlap. The presence of these genetic markers and the clinical pathway linking Glasdegib to Mitoxantrone supports the hypothesis that Glasdegib may exert a therapeutic effect in Myeloid Leukemia by influencing the same biological processes as Mitoxantrone. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Glasdegib to be a candidate for drug repositioning in this context, provided further mechanistic validation is conducted.",
